### Do the years 2017, 2018, 2019, and 2020 (the full years of Donald Trump's presidency) represent the lowest annual carbon emission figures within the 35-year period preceding the claim date of October 23, 2020?
Answer: No, the years 2017, 2018, 2019, and 2020 do not all represent the lowest annual carbon emission figures within the 35-year period preceding October 23, 2020. According to the search result, "US CO2 emissions in 2019 were the lowest they have been since 1992," meaning 2019 was the lowest in that period, but the source notes that emissions "went down in 2017 then shot back up in 2018 before falling again in 2019," indicating that 2018 was not a low point.

Source URL: https://www.climatechangenews.com/2020/10/06/us-emissions-four-years-president-trump/

### By the metric of year-over-year percentage decline, did the United States experience its largest annual drop in carbon emissions in 35 years during a year within the Trump administration (2017-2020)?
Answer: No, based on the search result, which details 2017 emissions data under the Trump administration, the annual decline in energy-related CO2 emissions was only 0.66%, which was "half their 2005-2016 average rate." This indicates a slowdown in the pace of reduction, not the largest annual drop in 35 years.

Source URL: https://rhg.com/research/final-us-emissions-numbers-for-2017/

